WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.600 --> 00:00:04.688
Welcome to part three, inside
the compressor and the turbine.

2
00:00:04.738 --> 00:00:09.106
We derive the Euler equation, fix the
conventions, analyse a compressor

3
00:00:09.146 --> 00:00:13.699
stage and the repeating-stage design
with its chart, solve exercises 2 A

4
00:00:13.739 --> 00:00:18.168
and 2 B, and then do the same for
turbine stages, with the design map,

5
00:00:18.208 --> 00:00:23.897
the Zweifel coefficient and the blade
stress check of exercises 3 A and 3 B.

6
00:00:23.947 --> 00:00:27.916
Exercise 3 A is also your
group 13 deliverable due on

7
00:00:27.956 --> 00:00:33.271
Wednesday the twenty-eighth at 17:00,
so pay special attention to it.

8
00:00:34.921 --> 00:00:37.454
Everything starts with Euler's equation.

9
00:00:37.504 --> 00:00:41.006
The torque on the fluid equals
the change of its angular

10
00:00:41.046 --> 00:00:44.865
momentum, m dot times r v at
exit minus r v at inlet, where v

11
00:00:44.905 --> 00:00:49.557
is the tangential velocity, and the
power is omega times the torque.

12
00:00:49.607 --> 00:00:52.150
Equating with the first law, the change of

13
00:00:52.190 --> 00:00:55.738
total enthalpy equals omega
times the change of r v.

14
00:00:55.788 --> 00:00:59.769
At the mean radius, with the blade
speed U equal to omega times

15
00:00:59.809 --> 00:01:03.726
r m, the compressor work per unit
mass is U times the increase

16
00:01:03.766 --> 00:01:08.201
of swirl, and the turbine work is
U times the decrease of swirl.

17
00:01:08.251 --> 00:01:11.577
So the work depends only on the
blade speed and the tangential

18
00:01:11.617 --> 00:01:15.757
velocities, not on the axial velocity
or the blade shape, and high blade

19
00:01:15.797 --> 00:01:20.490
speed is the most direct route to high
stage work, until blade stress stops you.

20
00:01:22.140 --> 00:01:27.099
Most mistakes in turbomachinery are
about conventions, so fix them now.

21
00:01:27.149 --> 00:01:31.027
A compressor stage is a rotor
followed by a stator, while a turbine

22
00:01:31.067 --> 00:01:35.004
stage is a stator, or nozzle,
followed by a rotor, so station 2 is

23
00:01:35.044 --> 00:01:38.747
the rotor exit in a compressor
but the rotor inlet in a turbine,

24
00:01:38.787 --> 00:01:43.114
and these local stations have nothing
to do with the cycle stations.

25
00:01:43.164 --> 00:01:47.621
Absolute and relative velocities
are related by V equals V R plus U,

26
00:01:47.661 --> 00:01:52.449
the axial component u is the same
in both frames, and all angles are

27
00:01:52.489 --> 00:01:57.667
measured from the axial direction,
alpha absolute and beta relative.

28
00:01:57.717 --> 00:02:00.901
Static properties are the same
in both frames, but total

29
00:02:00.941 --> 00:02:04.241
temperature, total pressure
and Mach number are not, which

30
00:02:04.281 --> 00:02:07.970
is why the rotor must always
be analysed in its own frame.

31
00:02:08.020 --> 00:02:11.926
In a compressor the tangential
components satisfy v plus v R

32
00:02:11.966 --> 00:02:16.134
equals U; in a turbine, following
Mattingly, v 2 is measured

33
00:02:16.174 --> 00:02:20.540
along U and v 3 against it, so
v 2 R equals v 2 minus U and

34
00:02:20.580 --> 00:02:25.402
v 3 R equals v 3 plus U, and
the two swirls add in the work.

35
00:02:27.052 --> 00:02:32.298
In a compressor stage, only the rotor
does work: c p delta T t equals U times v

36
00:02:32.338 --> 00:02:37.584
2 minus v 1, which can be written with
absolute angles, as adding swirl, or

37
00:02:37.624 --> 00:02:43.528
with relative angles, as removing relative
swirl, which is paid for with diffusion.

38
00:02:43.578 --> 00:02:46.902
Across the rotor, in its own
frame, nothing does work, so

39
00:02:46.942 --> 00:02:50.089
the relative total temperature
is conserved, while the

40
00:02:50.129 --> 00:02:53.901
relative total pressure drops
by the rotor loss ratio L R.

41
00:02:53.951 --> 00:02:59.352
Across the stator, total temperature is
conserved and total pressure drops by L S.

42
00:02:59.402 --> 00:03:02.909
The losses are quantified by
loss coefficients: the total

43
00:03:02.949 --> 00:03:06.519
pressure drop divided by the
inlet dynamic pressure in the

44
00:03:06.559 --> 00:03:10.439
row's frame, which converts into
L R equals one minus phi times

45
00:03:10.479 --> 00:03:15.808
gamma P 1 M 1 R squared over two P t
1 R, the form used in exercise 2 A.

46
00:03:15.858 --> 00:03:19.231
Both rows decelerate the flow
in their own frame, against an

47
00:03:19.271 --> 00:03:22.359
adverse pressure gradient,
and that is why a compressor

48
00:03:22.399 --> 00:03:26.332
needs many stages while one or
two turbine stages can drive it.

49
00:03:27.982 --> 00:03:31.595
A stage is characterised by
a few dimensionless groups.

50
00:03:31.645 --> 00:03:35.394
The stage pressure and temperature
ratios give its isentropic and

51
00:03:35.434 --> 00:03:40.040
polytropic efficiencies with the same
formulas as for a whole compressor.

52
00:03:40.090 --> 00:03:43.321
The degree of reaction is
the fraction of the static

53
00:03:43.361 --> 00:03:47.725
temperature rise that happens
in the rotor; 0.5 shares the

54
00:03:47.765 --> 00:03:52.317
diffusion equally, and Farokhi
argues for about 0.6 because

55
00:03:52.357 --> 00:03:56.984
rotating boundary layers tolerate
adverse gradients slightly better.

56
00:03:57.034 --> 00:04:00.579
The stage loading coefficient
Psi is c p delta T t over U

57
00:04:00.619 --> 00:04:06.615
squared, typically 0.30 to 0.35
in a high pressure compressor,

58
00:04:06.655 --> 00:04:13.104
and the flow coefficient Phi is u
over U, typically 0.45 to 0.55.

59
00:04:13.154 --> 00:04:16.370
The diffusion factor, due
to Lieblein, measures the

60
00:04:16.410 --> 00:04:20.002
deceleration on the suction
side and must stay below about

61
00:04:20.042 --> 00:04:26.403
0.6, while the de Haller ratio of
0.72 is only a rough sanity check.

62
00:04:26.453 --> 00:04:30.460
And the blade stress parameter, A
N squared, links the annulus area

63
00:04:30.500 --> 00:04:35.139
and the shaft speed to the specific
strength the material must provide.

64
00:04:36.789 --> 00:04:40.524
The mean-line analysis gives
flow angles, but the blades are

65
00:04:40.564 --> 00:04:45.254
drawn with metal angles, and the
cascade vocabulary connects the two.

66
00:04:45.304 --> 00:04:49.306
The solidity sigma is the chord
over the pitch, where the pitch is

67
00:04:49.346 --> 00:04:53.593
two pi r m over the number of
blades; referred to the axial chord

68
00:04:53.633 --> 00:04:58.576
it is the axial solidity, sigma times
the cosine of the stagger angle.

69
00:04:58.626 --> 00:05:01.577
The flow does not follow
the blade exactly, so we

70
00:05:01.617 --> 00:05:05.178
distinguish the flow angles,
alpha in the stator or beta in

71
00:05:05.218 --> 00:05:09.657
the rotor, from the metal angles
gamma, drawn dotted in the sketch.

72
00:05:09.707 --> 00:05:12.760
At the leading edge the
difference is the incidence,

73
00:05:12.800 --> 00:05:16.362
alpha i minus gamma i, which
is nearly zero at design.

74
00:05:16.412 --> 00:05:20.157
At the trailing edge the flow
under-turns by the deviation, alpha

75
00:05:20.197 --> 00:05:23.825
e minus gamma e: Carter's rule
gives the camber over four root

76
00:05:23.865 --> 00:05:27.552
sigma for a compressor, while in
a turbine the accelerating flow

77
00:05:27.592 --> 00:05:31.785
keeps it small, the sum of the
metal angles over eight root sigma.

78
00:05:31.835 --> 00:05:35.457
Packing the blades more closely
reduces deviation and the

79
00:05:35.497 --> 00:05:39.120
diffusion factor, at the price
of more blades, weight and

80
00:05:39.160 --> 00:05:44.457
friction; the Zweifel criterion of the
turbine fixes exactly this solidity.

81
00:05:46.107 --> 00:05:50.137
A powerful simplification is the
repeating-row, repeating-stage

82
00:05:50.177 --> 00:05:54.013
design: the stage exit velocity
and angle equal those at its

83
00:05:54.053 --> 00:05:58.926
inlet, using mirror-image rows, so
stages can be stacked indefinitely.

84
00:05:58.976 --> 00:06:03.770
Then v 1 plus v 2 equals the blade
speed, and the work becomes V 2

85
00:06:03.810 --> 00:06:08.268
squared minus V 1 squared over c
p: the same temperature rise for

86
00:06:08.308 --> 00:06:13.223
every stage, a consequence of the
geometry, not an extra assumption.

87
00:06:13.273 --> 00:06:15.724
The stage temperature
ratio depends only on

88
00:06:15.764 --> 00:06:18.778
the inlet Mach number
and the two flow angles.

89
00:06:18.828 --> 00:06:23.138
The ratio of wheel speed to inlet
velocity, and the loading and flow

90
00:06:23.178 --> 00:06:28.327
coefficients, become purely geometric
functions of alpha 1 and alpha 2.

91
00:06:28.377 --> 00:06:31.857
The degree of reaction is exactly
one half, and the polytropic

92
00:06:31.897 --> 00:06:35.151
efficiency of a stage equals
that of the whole compressor,

93
00:06:35.191 --> 00:06:39.175
which is why it is the efficiency
carried from stage to machine.

94
00:06:40.825 --> 00:06:44.527
Because the repeating stage
depends on only a few numbers, it

95
00:06:44.567 --> 00:06:48.966
is tabulated as a design chart,
like this one from exercise 2 A.

96
00:06:49.016 --> 00:06:53.680
Against the inlet angle alpha 1,
it shows the exit angle alpha 2 on

97
00:06:53.720 --> 00:06:58.054
the right axis, the wheel speed
ratio omega r over V 1 in green,

98
00:06:58.094 --> 00:07:02.949
and the stage pressure ratio for
several inlet Mach numbers in blue.

99
00:07:02.999 --> 00:07:07.066
There are two ways to use it: if
the blade speed is fixed by the

100
00:07:07.106 --> 00:07:11.557
shaft and the radius, choose alpha
1, read omega r over V 1, get V

101
00:07:11.597 --> 00:07:16.631
1 and the Mach number, and then the
stage pressure ratio and alpha 2.

102
00:07:16.681 --> 00:07:20.303
If instead a pressure ratio is the
target, every intersection with

103
00:07:20.343 --> 00:07:23.854
a Mach number line is a valid
design, which fixes the angles and

104
00:07:23.894 --> 00:07:28.072
the Mach number, and the chart then
tells you the blade speed needed.

105
00:07:28.122 --> 00:07:32.726
The trends: a higher inlet Mach number
gives more pressure ratio per stage,

106
00:07:32.766 --> 00:07:39.618
and a large alpha 1 requires a high wheel
speed; keep M 1 between 0.4 and 0.6.

107
00:07:41.268 --> 00:07:44.352
A multistage compressor
stacks identical repeating

108
00:07:44.392 --> 00:07:47.992
stages, each adding the same
total temperature rise.

109
00:07:48.042 --> 00:07:52.609
So the exit total temperature is the
inlet one plus n s times delta T

110
00:07:52.649 --> 00:07:56.682
t, and the overall pressure ratio
follows from the polytropic

111
00:07:56.722 --> 00:08:02.080
efficiency: the temperature ratio raised
to gamma e c over gamma minus one.

112
00:08:02.130 --> 00:08:05.390
Because the inlet temperature
of each stage grows, its

113
00:08:05.430 --> 00:08:09.057
temperature ratio and pressure
ratio fall along the machine,

114
00:08:09.097 --> 00:08:12.846
so the overall ratio is not the
first stage ratio to the power

115
00:08:12.886 --> 00:08:19.164
n s; in the manual's example the
stage ratio falls from 1.25 to 1.15.

116
00:08:19.214 --> 00:08:21.922
Inverting the formula
gives the number of stages

117
00:08:21.962 --> 00:08:25.403
for a target pressure ratio,
rounded up to an integer.

118
00:08:25.453 --> 00:08:29.227
The manual warns that Mattingly
also writes pi c equals pi

119
00:08:29.267 --> 00:08:32.532
c s to the N, but that is
only a device for relating

120
00:08:32.572 --> 00:08:36.926
efficiencies and is inconsistent
with real repeating triangles.

121
00:08:38.576 --> 00:08:43.616
So far everything happened at the mean
radius, but a blade spans from hub to tip.

122
00:08:43.666 --> 00:08:47.653
If the same swirl change were imposed
at every radius, Euler's equation

123
00:08:47.693 --> 00:08:51.793
would give a work proportional to the
radius, much larger at the tip, and

124
00:08:51.833 --> 00:08:56.323
the resulting pressure gradients would
drive recirculation at both walls.

125
00:08:56.373 --> 00:08:59.593
So the design keeps the work
uniform along the span, r

126
00:08:59.633 --> 00:09:02.611
times the swirl change
constant, combined with the

127
00:09:02.651 --> 00:09:06.382
radial equilibrium equation
for constant total enthalpy.

128
00:09:06.432 --> 00:09:10.268
The simplest solution is the
free vortex, r v constant, which

129
00:09:10.308 --> 00:09:14.399
keeps the axial velocity uniform
and gives a reaction of one

130
00:09:14.439 --> 00:09:18.601
minus one minus the mean reaction,
times r m over r squared.

131
00:09:18.651 --> 00:09:22.666
As the plot shows, the reaction is
lowest at the hub and highest at the

132
00:09:22.706 --> 00:09:26.663
tip, and on a long compressor blade
it can become negative at the hub,

133
00:09:26.703 --> 00:09:31.679
which is why other swirl distributions are
used when the hub to tip ratio is low.

134
00:09:31.729 --> 00:09:35.775
In a turbine the blades are shorter,
so the variation is milder, and

135
00:09:35.815 --> 00:09:41.332
the usual criterion is to keep the
hub reaction above about minus 0.15.

136
00:09:41.382 --> 00:09:44.643
Finally, the hub and tip
radii follow from the annulus

137
00:09:44.683 --> 00:09:48.067
area given by the mass flow
parameter and the chosen hub

138
00:09:48.107 --> 00:09:53.714
to tip ratio, typically 0.6 to
0.75 at a compressor inlet.

139
00:09:55.364 --> 00:09:58.696
Exercise 2 A gives the inlet
angle, the static inlet

140
00:09:58.736 --> 00:10:04.955
state and the shaft: 800 millimetres
mean radius at 2 500 rpm.

141
00:10:05.005 --> 00:10:08.801
The blade speed is omega
r: 2 500 times two pi

142
00:10:08.841 --> 00:10:14.945
over 60 times 0.8, which is
209.44 metres per second.

143
00:10:14.995 --> 00:10:19.014
Entering the chart at alpha 1 equal
to 30 degrees, the green line

144
00:10:19.054 --> 00:10:25.864
gives omega r over V 1 of about 1.6,
so V 1 is 130.9 metres per second.

145
00:10:25.914 --> 00:10:30.070
With the inlet speed
of sound of 346 metres

146
00:10:30.110 --> 00:10:34.217
per second, the inlet Mach number is 0.38.

147
00:10:34.267 --> 00:10:40.325
Now the triangle: the axial
velocity is V 1 cos 30, 113.36

148
00:10:40.365 --> 00:10:44.647
metres per second, the inlet
swirl is 65.45, and the

149
00:10:44.687 --> 00:10:51.846
repeating-row condition v 1 plus v 2
equals U gives v 2 equal to 143.99.

150
00:10:51.896 --> 00:10:57.508
So alpha 2 is 51.79 degrees, consistent
with the 50 degrees read on the

151
00:10:57.548 --> 00:11:04.541
chart, and V 2 is 183.26 metres per
second, all as in the official solution.

152
00:11:06.191 --> 00:11:08.999
Here are the two triangles
drawn to scale, with the

153
00:11:09.039 --> 00:11:13.577
tangential direction horizontal and
the axial direction pointing down.

154
00:11:13.627 --> 00:11:17.181
At the rotor inlet, the
absolute velocity V 1 and the

155
00:11:17.221 --> 00:11:20.840
relative velocity V 1 R start
from the same point, and

156
00:11:20.880 --> 00:11:25.412
the blade speed U joins their
tips, because V equals V R plus U.

157
00:11:25.462 --> 00:11:28.886
At the rotor exit the absolute
velocity has gained swirl,

158
00:11:28.926 --> 00:11:33.808
144 metres per second
against 65, and the relative

159
00:11:33.848 --> 00:11:38.026
velocity has lost it: the rotor
added work by turning the flow.

160
00:11:38.076 --> 00:11:41.372
Notice the mirror symmetry
of the repeating stage: the

161
00:11:41.412 --> 00:11:44.956
relative inlet velocity equals
the absolute exit velocity,

162
00:11:44.996 --> 00:11:50.949
183.3 metres per second, and the
relative exit velocity equals

163
00:11:50.989 --> 00:11:55.911
the absolute inlet velocity, so only
one triangle really has to be solved.

164
00:11:57.561 --> 00:12:00.337
Now the thermodynamics, row by row.

165
00:12:00.387 --> 00:12:05.445
The inlet total temperature is
306.53 kelvin and the total

166
00:12:05.485 --> 00:12:11.562
pressure 1.104 bar; in the rotor
frame, with V 1 R equal to 183

167
00:12:11.602 --> 00:12:18.918
metres per second, the relative totals
are 314.72 kelvin and 1.211 bar.

168
00:12:18.968 --> 00:12:22.424
The rotor loss coefficient
of 0.02 drops the

169
00:12:22.464 --> 00:12:26.275
relative total pressure
only to 1.207 bar, and

170
00:12:26.315 --> 00:12:29.238
since the relative total
temperature is conserved,

171
00:12:29.278 --> 00:12:35.967
the rotor exit static state is
306.19 kelvin and 1.096 bar.

172
00:12:36.017 --> 00:12:42.036
The stage work is V 2 squared minus V 1
squared over c p, 16.38 kelvin, so the

173
00:12:42.076 --> 00:12:50.624
absolute total temperature becomes 322.91
kelvin and the total pressure 1.320 bar.

174
00:12:50.674 --> 00:12:54.480
The stator loses a little
pressure, to 1.316 bar, and

175
00:12:54.520 --> 00:12:57.405
decelerates the flow back
to V 1, raising the static

176
00:12:57.445 --> 00:13:04.457
temperature to 314.38 kelvin and
the static pressure to 1.198 bar.

177
00:13:04.507 --> 00:13:11.476
So the stage pressure ratio is 1.192
with an isentropic efficiency of 96.4 %,

178
00:13:11.516 --> 00:13:18.006
the reaction is exactly 0.5, the
polytropic efficiency is 96.5 %, and nine

179
00:13:18.046 --> 00:13:25.465
such stages give an overall pressure ratio
of 3.77, the official 3.76 to rounding.

180
00:13:27.115 --> 00:13:30.052
The exercises ask for a T
s sketch of the stage, so

181
00:13:30.092 --> 00:13:33.769
here is a template drawn to
scale with the 2 A numbers.

182
00:13:33.819 --> 00:13:37.796
Start with the three static states on
their isobars: the static temperature

183
00:13:37.836 --> 00:13:42.470
rises through the rotor and again through
the stator, because both rows diffuse.

184
00:13:42.520 --> 00:13:46.749
Each total state sits above its
static state by V squared over two c

185
00:13:46.789 --> 00:13:51.659
p, at the same entropy, because a
total state is reached isentropically.

186
00:13:51.709 --> 00:13:56.678
In the rotor frame, the relative total
temperature is the same at 1 R and 2 R, a

187
00:13:56.718 --> 00:14:02.078
horizontal line, while the relative total
pressure drops a little by the loss ratio.

188
00:14:02.128 --> 00:14:04.907
In the absolute frame
the rotor adds work: the

189
00:14:04.947 --> 00:14:09.769
total temperature rises from
t 1 to t 2 by 16.4 kelvin.

190
00:14:09.819 --> 00:14:12.721
Through the stator the
absolute total temperature is

191
00:14:12.761 --> 00:14:15.946
constant and the total pressure
falls slightly, while the

192
00:14:15.986 --> 00:14:19.956
static temperature climbs back
as the flow decelerates to V 1.

193
00:14:20.006 --> 00:14:23.368
And every step moves to the
right: each loss is an entropy

194
00:14:23.408 --> 00:14:27.102
increase, which is how you
show the losses on the sketch.

195
00:14:28.752 --> 00:14:32.932
Exercise 2 B works the other way:
the target is an overall pressure

196
00:14:32.972 --> 00:14:37.642
ratio of 5 in nine stages, with
a polytropic efficiency of 90 %;

197
00:14:37.682 --> 00:14:43.758
careful, the statement gives an
inlet total temperature of 316.18

198
00:14:43.798 --> 00:14:50.998
kelvin, but the official solution uses
306.53, the value of 2 A, and so do we.

199
00:14:51.048 --> 00:14:55.113
On the 2 B chart, which plots the
nine-stage pressure ratio against

200
00:14:55.153 --> 00:14:59.633
alpha 1, any intersection of the pi
c equal to 5 level with a Mach line

201
00:14:59.673 --> 00:15:04.808
is valid; choosing M 1 equal to 0.45,
in the recommended range, gives

202
00:15:04.848 --> 00:15:10.671
alpha 1 about 26 degrees, and the dashed
line gives alpha 2 about 50 degrees.

203
00:15:10.721 --> 00:15:18.981
The static inlet temperature
is 306.53 over 1.0405, 294.6

204
00:15:19.021 --> 00:15:27.325
kelvin, so V 1 is 0.45 times
344, 154.82 metres per second.

205
00:15:27.375 --> 00:15:34.621
The axial velocity is V 1 cos 26, 139.15,
and the repeating-stage condition

206
00:15:34.661 --> 00:15:42.644
gives the blade speed needed, u times tan
26 plus tan 50, 233.71 metres per second.

207
00:15:42.694 --> 00:15:53.133
V 2 is u over cos 50, 216.48 metres per
second, with swirls of 67.87 and 165.84.

208
00:15:53.183 --> 00:16:00.116
And the stage work is V 2 squared minus
V 1 squared over c p, 22.79 kelvin.

209
00:16:01.766 --> 00:16:06.260
Now the stage cycle, row by row,
which the official sheet lists.

210
00:16:06.310 --> 00:16:11.034
The inlet static pressure is
