00:33:15.079 --> 00:33:22.093
Part 4, the stress check, uses the same
formula as 3 A, now at 25 000 rpm.

413
00:33:22.143 --> 00:33:25.117
In the strict design the
nozzle exit Mach number is

414
00:33:25.157 --> 00:33:31.595
1.79 and the mass flow parameter
for gamma 1.3 is 0.452.

415
00:33:31.645 --> 00:33:35.629
With the 1 A core flow
of 24.29 kilograms per

416
00:33:35.669 --> 00:33:39.925
second, the annulus area
is 0.061 square metres.

417
00:33:39.975 --> 00:33:44.128
So the required specific strength
is 60.2 megapascals per gram per

418
00:33:44.168 --> 00:33:48.756
cubic centimetre, close to the
official 57.7, which corresponds to

419
00:33:48.796 --> 00:33:54.147
their 0.059 square metres; at the
20 000 rpm printed on the official

420
00:33:54.187 --> 00:34:00.364
sheet it would be only 38.5, so that
number is a typo and 25 000 is right.

421
00:34:00.414 --> 00:34:04.587
The relaxed design has a lower
Mach number, 1.25, hence

422
00:34:04.627 --> 00:34:10.655
a larger annulus, 0.083 square
metres, and needs 81.4.

423
00:34:10.705 --> 00:34:13.765
The blades see a relative
total temperature of about 1

424
00:34:13.805 --> 00:34:17.805
405 kelvin, beyond the end
of every curve on the chart,

425
00:34:17.845 --> 00:34:20.849
so only a cooled single-crystal
nickel alloy works: the

426
00:34:20.889 --> 00:34:25.885
chart gives it about 55 to
57 near 1 200 kelvin, so the

427
00:34:25.925 --> 00:34:28.708
strict design is marginal
and needs the metal below

428
00:34:28.748 --> 00:34:33.412
about 1 180 kelvin, while the
relaxed one would need it

429
00:34:33.452 --> 00:34:37.898
near 1 050 kelvin; the official
answer is single crystal.

430
00:34:39.548 --> 00:34:43.093
Before leaving the turbines,
a few sanity checks that the

431
00:34:43.133 --> 00:34:47.131
exercises do not ask for but
that show real understanding.

432
00:34:47.181 --> 00:34:52.831
In 3 A, a blade speed of 656
metres per second at 2 500

433
00:34:52.871 --> 00:34:56.944
rpm means a mean radius of U
over omega, 2.51 metres, and

434
00:34:56.984 --> 00:35:01.056
with an annulus of 0.23 square
metres the blades would be

435
00:35:01.096 --> 00:35:05.503
only about 1.5 centimetres tall,
which is not a real turbine.

436
00:35:05.553 --> 00:35:09.975
Its nozzle exit Mach number of
1.11 also comes with a rotor exit

437
00:35:10.015 --> 00:35:18.825
relative Mach number of 707 over 584,
1.21, above the 0.9 the manual allows.

438
00:35:18.875 --> 00:35:23.486
The strict 3 B design is
worse: M 2 of 1.79 would need

439
00:35:23.526 --> 00:35:29.384
convergent-divergent vanes, and the
rotor exit relative Mach number is 1.42.

440
00:35:29.434 --> 00:35:35.082
The relaxed 3 B design, with M 2 of 1.25
and a rotor exit relative Mach number

441
00:35:35.122 --> 00:35:40.829
of 0.74, is aerodynamically better,
but it needs a specific strength of 81

442
00:35:40.869 --> 00:35:46.212
instead of 60, so a designer would keep it
only with a lower speed or more cooling.

443
00:35:46.262 --> 00:35:50.655
The manual's section on the optimal
nozzle exit swirl Mach number ties

444
00:35:50.695 --> 00:35:55.595
these together: with zero exit swirl,
M 2 sin alpha 2 equals 2 M T times

445
00:35:55.635 --> 00:36:00.029
one minus R t, where M T is the blade
speed over the speed of sound at

446
00:36:00.069 --> 00:36:04.652
the nozzle exit, so the reaction and
the blade Mach number set the swirl,

447
00:36:04.692 --> 00:36:09.983
and the nozzle exit Mach number is chosen
for maximum work per unit mass flow.

448
00:36:10.033 --> 00:36:13.457
In the exam, solve exactly
what the statement asks, and

449
00:36:13.497 --> 00:36:16.984
then add one line of sanity
check like these: it costs a

450
00:36:17.024 --> 00:36:20.901
minute and shows the examiner
you understand the result.

451
00:36:22.551 --> 00:36:25.322
To finish, here is what I
would put on the second

452
00:36:25.362 --> 00:36:28.695
sheet, pages three and four,
for the turbomachinery.

453
00:36:28.745 --> 00:36:32.596
Euler for both machines with the
sign conventions, and the frame

454
00:36:32.636 --> 00:36:37.303
relation V equals V R plus U with
angles from the axial direction.

455
00:36:37.353 --> 00:36:39.650
The compressor row relations with the loss

456
00:36:39.690 --> 00:36:42.823
coefficient conversion and
the degree of reaction.

457
00:36:42.873 --> 00:36:46.845
The repeating-stage formulas and
the multistage pressure ratio.

458
00:36:46.895 --> 00:36:50.926
The turbine loading and flow
coefficients, the map relations, the

459
00:36:50.966 --> 00:36:55.372
reaction cases and the 70 degree
limit, plus the Zweifel and A N

460
00:36:55.412 --> 00:36:59.958
squared formulas and the annulus
area with the mass flow parameter.

461
00:37:00.008 --> 00:37:04.317
Add one line for the radial variation
and the cascade vocabulary.

462
00:37:04.367 --> 00:37:08.460
And since the exam is about method,
copy the station table of 1 A and

463
00:37:08.500 --> 00:37:12.709
the triangles and T s templates of
2 A and 3 A as worked templates;

464
00:37:12.749 --> 00:37:17.349
then practise each exercise once more
without looking, against the clock.

465
00:37:17.399 --> 00:37:18.434
Good luck.
